# Educational prompts are instrument-specific; availability and curriculum are confirmed by the school.
lesson_details = {
 'piano': ('Two hands.<br>One musical idea.', 'Explore how melody and accompaniment fit together across the keyboard.', 'A place to practice', 'A keyboard or piano you can use regularly makes it easier to revisit a lesson. Ask about key size, a pedal, and seating before investing in equipment. Bring a song you would enjoy learning.', 'Try a simple melody slowly, then notice how the other hand can add a steady accompaniment. Ask your teacher how to build coordination without rushing.'),
 'guitarlessons': ('A chord.<br>A favorite song.', 'Make a connection between the rhythm you hear and the way you strum.', 'Comfort before speed', 'The guitar should feel manageable to hold and fret. Let the school know if you are choosing a smaller instrument or returning after a break. A comfortable setup matters more than extra accessories.', 'Choose a short chord change and keep the rhythm even. Bring a recording or song title so your teacher can discuss a practical starting point.'),
 'electric-guitar': ('Find your tone.<br>Shape your sound.', 'Explore the relationship between touch, rhythm and an amplified sound.', 'Plan your setup', 'An electric guitar needs a suitable amplifier or practice setup. Before your visit, ask whether to bring your own cable and amp. Tell your teacher about the styles and sounds that interest you.', 'A short riff can be a useful way to discuss clean fretting, picking and muting. Start slowly and pay attention to unwanted string noise as well as the notes you mean to play.'),
 'bass-guitar': ('Keep the pulse.<br>Connect the band.', 'Listen for the part that gives a song its rhythmic foundation.', 'Get comfortable with the bass', 'Share whether you use a four-string or another bass, and ask about an amplifier for lessons. A comfortable strap and instrument position can help you focus on timing and sound.', 'Listen to how a bass line works with the drums. A repeating pattern gives you a way to explore steady time, note length and clear changes between strings.'),
 'violin': ('Listen closely.<br>Let the bow sing.', 'Explore the small adjustments that help a string speak clearly.', 'Start with instrument fit', 'A violin that fits the player is an important starting point. Ask the school about sizing, bow condition and a comfortable support setup. Bring school-orchestra music if you would like to discuss it.', 'A short phrase offers a chance to work on bow direction, listening and reading together. Tell your teacher which part feels difficult so you can make practice more focused.'),
 'viola': ('A richer register.<br>Your own voice.', 'Discover the viola’s sound and its place between melody and harmony.', 'Moving to viola or continuing?', 'Tell us whether you are new to strings, moving from violin, or already playing viola. Discuss instrument size and the music you read, including any questions about alto clef.', 'Explore the sound of a simple phrase across different strings. Reading, comfortable bowing and listening to the ensemble can all shape the part you play.'),
 'cello': ('A grounded setup.<br>A singing line.', 'Connect a comfortable seated position with an expressive sound.', 'Prepare your playing space', 'Ask about cello size, chair height and an endpin stop before your first lesson. Share any transport questions and whether you have a suitable place to practice at home.', 'Start with a small passage and listen to how each note connects. A teacher can help you separate questions about the bow, left hand and rhythm into manageable practice steps.'),
 'upright-bass': ('A steady foundation.<br>A full sound.', 'Explore how an individual bass part supports the whole ensemble.', 'Coordinate the practical details', 'The upright bass needs room and a workable transport plan. Confirm which instrument will be available at your lesson and discuss size, bow or pizzicato interests, and the music you want to play.', 'A simple line is a useful starting point for exploring pitch, pulse and note length. Tell your teacher about your ensemble so the conversation relates to the music you are making.'),
 'ukulele': ('A few chords.<br>A song to share.', 'Bring a favorite tune and explore a comfortable rhythm for it.', 'Know your instrument', 'Ukuleles come in different sizes and tunings. Tell the school which one you have, or ask for guidance before buying. Bring your questions about tuning, holding the instrument and getting started.', 'Try a short chord pattern at an easy pace. Singing or counting along can help you notice where the changes happen and keep your strumming connected to the song.')
}

